## Most of the time you don't need them
Single-document operations are atomic, and good modelling (embedding) keeps related data in one document. Reach for a multi-document transaction only when you truly must change several documents or collections together, for example moving money between two account documents.

## Multi-document ACID transactions
Available on replica sets (4.0+) and sharded clusters (4.2+).

```js
const session = client.startSession();
await session.withTransaction(async () => {
  await accounts.updateOne({ _id: from }, { $inc: { bal: -amt } }, { session });
  await accounts.updateOne({ _id: to },   { $inc: { bal:  amt } }, { session });
}, { readConcern: { level: 'snapshot' }, writeConcern: { w: 'majority' } });
```

`withTransaction` **retries** automatically on transient errors (`TransientTransactionError`) and unknown commit results. Keep transactions short (they have a time limit and hold snapshot resources), touch few documents, and avoid long reads inside them. They carry a performance cost, so don't wrap every write.

## Conflicts
MongoDB uses optimistic, snapshot-based concurrency: if two transactions modify the same document, one gets a **write conflict** and must retry.

## Causal consistency
In a distributed setup a read after your own write might hit a secondary that has not replicated it yet. A **causally consistent session** guarantees you read your own writes and see operations in the order that respects causality, using `readConcern: majority` with `writeConcern: majority`.

## Idempotent writes and uniqueness
Use unique indexes and `upsert` with a deterministic `_id` to make retries safe. Combine with the **outbox pattern**: write the business change and an outbox document in one transaction, and a separate process publishes the event.

## Compared with SQL
Joins, constraints and cross-table transactions are first-class in SQL. In Mongo you trade some of that for flexible schema and horizontal scale, and move more integrity logic into application code and careful modelling.
